178:PROVED correction to the proposed quasirandomness step: ordinary asymptotic C4 accuracy at o(n⁴) only implies residual eigenvalues o(n), not a bound at sqrt(n). An additive Cn³ residual-fourth-moment bound is sufficient for a constant-fraction sqrt(n)-scale bulk; a bare o(n⁴) statement is not. More explicitly, for a fixed alpha in (0,1), divide n vertices into r~n^(1/3) equal groups of size s~n^(2/3), take a symmetric independent-edge density-alpha graph B on the r groups, and replace each group by s twins (no internal edges). With positive probability tr B²~alpha r² and lambda1(B)~alpha r, so the residual square mass is ~alpha(1−alpha)r². The C4 law is alpha⁴r⁴+o(r⁴), hence after blowing up it is alpha⁴n⁴+o(n⁴), but every nonzero blown-up eigenvalue has form s lambda(B). Since s/sqrt(n)->infinity, the energy inside gamma sqrt(n) is at most r gamma² n=o(n²), while a constant fraction of n² remains outside the top. This is a generic-graph logical counterexample to the proposed inference, not an assertion that these graphs have the self-difference form.
180:PROVED justification of the probabilistic base just used: center B by alpha(J−I). In the fourth trace of this centered matrix, independence and mean zero kill every closed walk using an edge only once, leaving O(r³) walks with bounded expectation. Thus its expected fourth trace is O(r³). Markov gives centered operator norm o(r) with probability tending to one. The edge count is alpha r²+o(r²) by its variance O(r²). It follows that lambda1(B)=alpha r+o(r), every other eigenvalue is o(r), and tr B⁴=alpha⁴r⁴+o(r⁴). This supplies the required qualitative law without importing a quantitative random-matrix theorem.
